olumn vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5x+0y=10 \\\\ -3x+2y=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5x=10 \\\\ -3x+2y=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299629 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y+0z=3 \\\\ -2x+0y+6z=9 \\\\ 0x+7y-5z=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299630 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+0y+4z=10 \\\\ 2x-5y+0z=-5 \\\\ -4x+3y+7z=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299671 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=6i-8j,\\,v=-8i-j,\\,a=-2i+j,\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ -8i-j & =(6i-8j)+(-2i+j)t \\\\ -14i+7j & =(-2i+j)t \\\\ -14i+7j & =-2ti+tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-14=-2t \\\\ 7=t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,t=7\\,\\text{h}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299729 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=-6i-5j,\\,v=10i+19j,\\,a=2i+3j,\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 10i+19j & =-6i-5j+(2i+3j)t \\\\ 16i+24j & =(2i+3j)t \\\\ 16i+24j & =2ti+3tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}16=2t \\\\ 24=3t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,t=8\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299730 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=10i-8j,\\,v=-8i+28j,\\,a=-2i+4j,\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ -8i+28j & =10i-8j+(-2i+4j)t \\\\ -18i+36j & =(-2i+4j)t \\\\ -18i+36j & =-2ti+4tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-18=-2t \\\\ 36=4t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,t=9\\,\\text{h}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299904 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x-y=5 \\\\ x+3y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299905 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y=7 \\\\ x+2y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299909 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x+0y=-8 \\\\ x-3y=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299910 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+3y=5 \\\\ x+4y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299911 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-1 \\\\ 4x+y=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302065 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+0y=8 \\\\ -3x+4y=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3\\text{ } & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302066 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0x+7y=-14 \\\\ -5x+2y=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 2-(-5)\\cdot 7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302067 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=3 \\\\ 0x+5y=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 5-0\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302108 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y=y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302109 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & k+2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=3 \\\\  & (k+2)y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nNow if $k=-2$, then the second equation is $0=-2,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when  $k=-2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302143 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & k & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & k & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & k-2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4x-y=2 \\\\ (k-2)y=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=2,$ then the second equation is $0=9,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302193 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now   \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}=4 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ Since we have that ${x}_{1}=4$,  we only need to find ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-6-2{x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302218 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-4$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and ${x}_{1}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-4+3(-\\frac{2}{3})=-6.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302267 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3-(-3) & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3-(-3)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-6{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302268 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5-5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\\,t{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302280 "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ -{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=7 \\\\ -{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the result to the  first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3{c}_{2}=9\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{c}_{1}+3=1\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302281 "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5{c}_{1}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2\\cdot 1+{c}_{2}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302282 "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5{c}_{2}=10\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2\\cdot 2=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302283 "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=4 \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{c}_{1}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2+{c}_{2}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302304 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=2 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=2$ and ${y}_{2}=6.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302308 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=3 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =3, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-1+4{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-1+4(3) \\\\  & =11, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =4-3{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =4-3(3)-2(11) \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302319 "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-5 \\\\ {c}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=3.$ Substituting into the first equation, we obtain ${c}_{1}=-8.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302324 "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1/4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2(0)} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ 4{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}=-3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186302338 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=7 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{align