{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta -8\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-12\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\beta -8\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -8\\beta =10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{15}{26},\\beta =-\\frac{5}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{15}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(-\\frac{15}{26})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-\\frac{5}{13})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186305128 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n7\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+14{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-4\\operat