\\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+3=5, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+(-4)=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ |  |  | $-5$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈5$\nand\n$z(1)≈-5.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308427 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1-x+2y+z=1-1+2\\cdot (-1)+1=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+{z}^{2}=1-(-1)+{1}^{2}=3, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-1)\\cdot 1=-1, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+(-1)=-2, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+3=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $-2$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1-x+2y+z=1-2+2\\cdot (-2)+4=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+{z}^{2}=2-(-2)+{4}^{2}=20, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-1)\\cdot 1=-1, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=20\\cdot 1=20, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+(-1)=-3, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=4+20=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $20$ | $20$ |\n| $3$ | $-3$ |  |  | $24$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈-3$\nand\n$z(3)≈24.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308428 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=-2$ and asked to find their values at $x=0.$ The distance from $x=-2$ to $x=0$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-2)}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-2)=0$ and $z(-2)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+{y}^{2}-z=-2+{0}^{2}-1=-3, \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2-y+2z=2-0+2\\cdot 1=4, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-3)\\cdot 1=-3, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-2+1=-1, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+(-3)=-3, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+4=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $-1$ | $-3$ |  |  | $5$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+{y}^{2}-z=-1+(-3)^{2}-5=3, \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2-y+2z=2-(-3)+2\\cdot 5=15, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=15\\cdot 1=15, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-3+3=0, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=5+15=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $-1$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $5$ | $15$ | $15$ |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(0)≈0$\nand\n$z(0)≈20.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308435 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ y={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | $4.48$ | $1$ | $0.22$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/39/3981c3c5bdfc66850798ee3c34031acb0d1cdfea7181eb34a0a0422b44cc2ef0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f2b5b95167b1ecd07aa1152aeac6dd454fbe71ba953b05c4da22d8c36f76277a.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186308450 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(3\\beta -2\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-3\\alpha -2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\beta -2\\alpha =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -3\\alpha -2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\beta =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{2\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =2 \\\\ A-2-0 & =2 \\\\ A & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=4{e}^{2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308451 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(3\\beta +4\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\beta +4\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -3\\alpha +4\\beta =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\beta =4.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-4\\cdot 0}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =2 \\\\ A-3-0 & =2 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{-4x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308460 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′} & =4x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =4x-3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4x-3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{4x-3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$ So, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{4x-3yz}{x},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=2$ and $z(1)=1,$ we place $y=2$ and $z=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4x-3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-6}{1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-2$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+1=3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-2)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈3.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308462 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+6y & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =x{y}^{′}-6y+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=xz-6y+{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(2)={y}^{′}(2)=2.$ So, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(2)=-3, \\\\ {z}^{′}=xz-6y+{x}^{2},\\, & z(2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(2)=-3$ and $z(2)=2,$ we place $y=-3$ and $z=2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =xz-6y+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-6\\cdot (-3)+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4+18+4 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ |  | $2$ | $26$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =26\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $26$ | $26$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-3+2=-1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+26=28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $26$ | $26$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ |  |  | $28$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈-1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308469 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6ec9eac46644188b8b41a1a7ea28ca0542ee7e5c69d713028481904e90dd557a.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3t{e}^{4t} \\\\ y=3(t+1){e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.20$ | $0$ | $0.45$ |\n| $y$ | $0.20$ | $3$ | $4.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/b0/b0be26a1544bbb3117afab15d004bafee70d831fffab4b1f6921870871ac4768.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/64/64e685f4ea06a8f497ddfbe50a092a0362b8c92a9d2f78f9da2a8164113a785a.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186308470 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{0} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{3(0)}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=3 \\\\ {c}_{2}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=2.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=5.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308471 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/55/558ff2994d715139bde11747fdffddbbbd20f17f80c8deb3b90f4ae76078bdba.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ y=(2t+1){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | $0.00$ | $1$ | $0.45$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/10/10050fff096c999bdd1d845a247dac87a3135458313338f62018612639a011b0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c2/c227e135e04b76f093d8c2ad6e2a4365e206c19b281f1a3df7a4b65dd479f411.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186308472 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{t}^{2}+2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-(0)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{3(0)}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-(0)^{2}+2 \\\\ (0)^{2}-0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+2 \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2=8 \\\\ {c}_{2}=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=6.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=-7.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=6\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-7\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{t}^{2}+2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308473 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2}-3t \\\\ {t}^{2}+t+1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)} \\\\ {e}^{2(0)}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot {0}^{2}-3\\cdot 0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+0+1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}+1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=-3 \\\\ {c}_{2}+1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=-3.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=-3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2}-3t \\\\ {t}^{2}+t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308474 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-1 \\\\ -2t+3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{0}^{2}-1 \\\\ -2\\cdot 0+3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}-1 \\\\ {c}_{2}+3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-1=10 \\\\ {c}_{2}+3=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=11.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=9.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=11\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+9\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-1 \\\\ -2t+3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308478 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+4y{y}^{′}-3y & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =3{x}^{2}-4y{y}^{′}+3y \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4y{y}^{′}+3y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$ So, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x},\\, & z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=0,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {1}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0+3}{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+3=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308479 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}(2t-1){e}^{-2t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ {e}^{-2(0)}\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}(2\\cdot 0-1){e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 0\\cdot {e}^{-2(0)}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+1 \\\\ {c}_{1}+3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+1=-1 \\\\ {c}_{1}+3=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{1}=3.$  Substituting this into the second equation and solving for ${c}_{2},$ we get ${c}_{2}=8.$ \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}(2t+1){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308480 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0){e}^{-0} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0){e}^{-0}\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0){e}^{-0} \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0){e}^{-0}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+3 \\\\ -{c}_{2}+2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+3=7 \\\\ -{c}_{2}+2=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=4.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}-5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308482 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}-2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=4 \\\\ {c}_{2}-2=1\\end{aligned}\n$