:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-2xz-3{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =1-4-3 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  | $2$ | $-6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+(-3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308487 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+{y}^{3} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={x}^{3}+x{y}^{′}-{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}={x}^{3}+xz-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(0)={y}^{′}(0)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(0)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}={x}^{3}+xz-{y}^{3},\\, & z(0)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=1,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={x}^{3}+xz-{y}^{3} \\\\  & ={0}^{3}+0\\cdot 1-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308496 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}-3y{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3}{2}-1=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-3}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=z(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308497 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t)={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtraction the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{4t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308498 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $3.54$ | $0$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/94/9446137440c19f9aca73c9c666089ecff5a7712ee3f389ab891a150c13ffa780.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)|\\le 1$ and $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)|\\le 1.$ Thus, the obtained curve is $\\text{bounded}.$ So, we have $\\text{an ellipse}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/06/0678458809cf39967d7dda0d1ffbe4d43541ec309266bd08da6245ec23f6439d.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186308501 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ y={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.52$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.52$ | $0.53$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/2b/2b273edc4f977f2bee4d9a8c41128d4f2071ec39b0840610cbb7ee44492aae3e.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)|\\le 1,$ $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)|\\le 1,$ and ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, the curve $\\text{converges}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we have $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nAgain, since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c001f8a99c09e3d1da8c6107a298989c03e64ec58c23319ce0837a757c5ddafd.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186308503 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n2(2x+y)=4x+2y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -2k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308506 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+y=0 \\\\ 6x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -3k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-3x.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308507 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+2y=0 \\\\ -2x+4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-x+2y=0\\,⇒\\,2y=x\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ \\frac{k}{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{x}{2}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308508 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y=0 \\\\ 8x-4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 2k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=2x.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308509 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 2x+4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2y=0\\,⇒\\,2y=-x\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -\\frac{k}{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-\\frac{x}{2}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308510 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y=0 \\\\ -6x+8y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-3x+4y=0\\,⇒\\,4y=3x\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{3}{4}x.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308511 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ y{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}+4x{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4x{y}^{′}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xz}{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xz}{y},\\, & z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xz}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 0}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈1$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308512 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{y}^{″}-({y}^{′})^{2} & =4x \\\\ 3y{y}^{″} & =4x+({y}^{′})^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4x+({y}^{′})^{2}}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{5}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |  |  | $\\frac{8}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈2$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈\\frac{8}{3}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308513 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=3,$ we place $y=-1$ and $z=3$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6x+2y+z \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+3 \\\\  & =6-2+3 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x-y+z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-(-1)+3 \\\\  & =2+1+3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $7$ |  | $3$ | $6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $3$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+7=6, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=3+6=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $3$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $6$ |  |  | $9$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈6$ and $z(2)≈9.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308514 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=3$ and $z(0)=-2,$ we place $y=3$ and $z=-2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x+2y+z \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3+(-2) \\\\  & =0+6-2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x-y+3z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-3+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0-3-6 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $4$ |  | $-2$ | $-9$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-9)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $4$ | $8$ | $-2$ | $-9$ | $-18$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+8=11 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+(-18)=-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $4$ | $8$ | $-2$ | $-9$ | $-18$ |\n| $2$ | $11$ |  |  | $-20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈11$\nand\n$z(2)≈-20.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308521 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-2y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =2y-x{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2y-x{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{4})}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{15}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{18}=\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{9}$ | $\\frac{5}{18}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |  |  | $\\frac{25}{9}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈\\frac{15}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈\\frac{25}{9}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308522 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =y-4{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y-4{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{-1-4\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}}=-9 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-9\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+1=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2-\\frac{9}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0-4(-\\frac{5}{2})}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{10}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=\\frac{40}{9} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{40}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0-\\frac{5}{4}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{20}{9}=-\\frac{5}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{40}{9}$ | $\\frac{20}{9}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{18}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈-\\frac{5}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈-\\frac{5}{18}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308524 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-0=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x+2y-z}{(x-z)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+2\\cdot 4-2}{(0-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+8-2}{(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =xy+2z \\\\  & =0\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  | $2$ | $4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+3=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+8=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ |  |  | $10$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈7$\nand\n$z(2)≈10.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308525 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-1=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-y+2z \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-3+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-3+2 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x+y+z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3+1 \\\\  & =2+3+1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $2$ |  | $1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+2=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+4=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+12=13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |  | $13$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈7$\nand\n$z(3)≈13.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308532 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t)={e}^{-4t} \\\\ r(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Substituting ${\\alpha }_{0}=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+1={e}^{-4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-6(-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1))t+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4}({e}^{-4t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({e}^{-4t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3+3{e}^{-4t}+2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+3{e}^{-4t}}{2} \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2(-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1))t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4}({e}^{-4t}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-4t}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-4t}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1+3{e}^{-4t} & -3+3{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-4t} & 3-{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308533 "Explanation:\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t)={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t} \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding to the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3(-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =-({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((-4{e}^{-2t}+4{e}^{t})+({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t} & -2{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308538 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{″}+5({y}^{′})^{2}+x & =0 \\\\ 2x{y}^{″} & =-5({y}^{′})^{2}-x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{5({y}^{′})^{2}+x}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x},\\, & z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2$ and $z(1)=-1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x}=-\\frac{5\\cdot (-1)^{2}+1}{2\\cdot 1}=-3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-3\\cdot 1=-3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2-1=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1-3=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x}=-\\frac{5\\cdot (-4)^{2}+2}{2\\cdot 2}=-\\frac{41}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-4\\cdot 1=-4 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{41}{2}\\cdot 1=-\\frac{41}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1-4=-3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-4-\\frac{41}{2}=-\\frac{49}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ | $-\\frac{41}{2}$ | $-\\frac{41}{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $-3$ |  |  | $-\\frac{49}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈-3$\nand\n${y}^{′}(3)=z(3)≈-\\frac{49}{2}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308540 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-x & =0 \\\\ y{y}^{″} & =x-3{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{x-3{y}^{′}}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{x-3z}{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(-1)={y}^{′}(-1)=-2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(-1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{x-3z}{y},\\, & z(-1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=-1$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=1$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=1$ and $z(-1)=-2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x-3z}{y}=\\frac{-1-3(-2)}{1}=5 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-2\\cdot 1=-2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=5\\cdot 1=5 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1-2=-1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+5=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x-3z}{y}=\\frac{0-3\\cdot 3}{(-1)}=9 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=9\\cdot 1=9 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+3=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=3+9=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $9$ | $9$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $12$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈2$\nand\n${y}^{′}(1)=z(1)≈12.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308609 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-5\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((5\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(5\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(5\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+5\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308611 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-3t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-3t)={e}^{-3t} \\\\ r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-3t} \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-6t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308612 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{4},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting $p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp-{p}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp-{p}^{4})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-4{p}^{3}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-4{p}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x-4{p}^{3}=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=4{p}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp-{p}^{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =(4{p}^{3})p-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =4{p}^{4}-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =3{p}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4{p}^{3} \\\\ y=3{p}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np={(\\frac{x}{4})}^{1/3}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3{p}^{4} \\\\  & =3{({(\\frac{x}{4})}^{1/3})}^{4} \\\\  & =3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308620 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\sqrt{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+\\sqrt{p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}}=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+\\sqrt{p}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+\\sqrt{p} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}})p+\\sqrt{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{p}{2\\sqrt{p}}+\\sqrt{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{p}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}} \\\\ y=\\frac{\\sqrt{p}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $\\sqrt{p}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{p}=-\\frac{1}{2x}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}},$ we must have $x<0.$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $\\sqrt{p}$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{p}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2x})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{4x},\\,x<0.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308621 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+2({y}^{′})^{2}+3,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+2{p}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+2{p}^{2}+3)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+4p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+4p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+4p=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-4p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+2{p}^{2}+3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =(-4p)p+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-4{p}^{2}+2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4p \\\\ y=-2{p}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$ \n\n$$\np=-\\frac{x}{4}.\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2{p}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-2{(-\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=3-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308622 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p).\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p))^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p){p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p).\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p))p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p) \\\\  & =p\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p) \\\\ y=p\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nThis is valid only for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le p\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =p\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)\\cdot x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)),\\,-1\\le x\\le 1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308634 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(5t)={e}^{5t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(5t)={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}5t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{5t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n5t{e}^{5t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}(1-5t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5{e}^{5t}t+{e}^{5t}(1-5t) \\\\  & ={e}^{5t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{5t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308635 "The point $P(1,2,1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+2\\,j+k \\\\ t\\,i+({t}^{3}+1)\\,j+{t}^{5}\\,k & =i+2\\,j+k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ {t}^{5}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satifies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,2,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5})\\,k \\\\  & =i+3{t}^{2}\\,j+5{t}^{4}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1) & =i+3(1)^{2}\\,j+5(1)^{4}\\,k \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+5\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(1,2,1)$ is \n\n$$\nR(u)=i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308644 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ r(4t)={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3{\\alpha }_{0}=4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}), \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =3(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((3{e}^{4t}-3{e}^{t})+(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2{e}^{4t}+{e}^{t}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{4t}-2{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}-{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{4t}+{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308645 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=0,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(0)={e}^{0} \\\\ r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=1 \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the first equation, we immediately get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Substituting ${\\alpha }_{0}=1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt{\\alpha }_{1}+1={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-(\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1))t+1 \\\\  & =-({e}^{t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}+2 \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & =(\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1))t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t}+2 & {e}^{t}-1 \\\\ 2-2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t}-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308651 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5afce1bca483dbd9bd462810c351d710469b744813cd9f54ea3234f0c7343d35.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+1 \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/f7/f7be54fdf4b1cbe65be9730989c06b934604bc8f0b4300bd6b616ece9de7b8f5.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t-1 \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/47/47c41f2d62aea217d56259bf9918b1eba5bed3141d2bcfcfd486241bc32df048.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/57/57684286e917c2dcbcb6f6a7f19a82d5603c1fe11a39cb8a6b12473cce5105f5.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186308652 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/58/58564c40fcde276fb69732ce5926b7e350fe8963b51b21df0ce4de39e8481514.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d0/d03f88d2eab48a0557f05311cd0d13d956b2af954277d322fabc4091f3aef2d4.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/e0/e0748b246241a44132c526d1419d301ec67579c1d1cfbac0854c8bfccea67dff.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/66/66a32254dcc579eb248d8691d0c079815e29b59c650fc4b38eaa47bfb1525ddc.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186308654 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 8{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(1+4\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(1-4\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((1+4\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(1+4\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(1+4\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ ({\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+4{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t+{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308655 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(-1+2\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(-1-2\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((-1+2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(-1+2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(-1+2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ (-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+2{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308656 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -10{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(2+5\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(2-5\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2+5\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2+5\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2+5\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ (2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+5{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t+{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308657 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{3\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot 3\\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+3{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308658 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-4\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(4\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{4\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot 4\\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+4{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308659 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot \\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308660 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -13 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -13{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-5\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(5\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{5\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot 5\\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+5{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-13{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-13\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308661 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a70188cf5de7e7b64530aa661447b7fa7fc1e918ca3de41088e67274d813b7a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3t \\\\ y=9t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/37/37deb5a0fda7ef15a016ec2d73a3b935074b6696184dd480ebe5b858ce517500.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t \\\\ y=-9t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/60/60b49680cf94888122f25432586a3546dea206ae960f951afd187444afc8881a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186308662 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bff5cf9d21f0b01571b577ebe3d98ef08525d13a70a1d7379b537f6ba5319613.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4t+3 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/7d/7d590ee38f7f1b6b43c9b650890f42fa31b4c15951dab8bcb003b1ac1cc45c39.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4t-3 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/b9/b965e3e244bc49b5e0f52840550c2ae997a0e38dc40e8757f0f51905c448d7af.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186308670 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(3t)={e}^{3t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{e}^{3t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(1-3t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6{e}^{3t}t+{e}^{3t}(1-3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(1+3t), \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3{e}^{3t}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+3t & -3t \\\\ 3t & 1-3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308671 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -9{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\nt{e}^{t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(1-t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-9{e}^{t}t \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4{e}^{t}t+{e}^{t}(1-t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(1+3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1-3t & t \\\\ -9t & 1+3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308672 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 12{\\alpha }_{1}t & -4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(2t)={e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n2t{e}^{2t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(1-2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =8{e}^{2t}t+{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(1+6t) \\\\ 12{\\alpha }_{1}t & =12{e}^{2t}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+6t & -3t \\\\ 12t & 1-6t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308673 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=6t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(6t)={e}^{6t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(6t)={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{6t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n6t{e}^{6t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t}(1-6t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(-t), \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1-t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+t & t \\\\ -t & 1-t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308674 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 8{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(3t)={e}^{3t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{e}^{3t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(1-3t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2{e}^{3t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(-2t) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-{e}^{3t}t+{e}^{3t}(1-3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(1-4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+4t & -2t \\\\ 8t & 1-4t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308675 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\nt{e}^{t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(1-t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6{e}^{t}t+{e}^{t}(1-t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(1+5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5{e}^{t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+5t & -5t \\\\ 5t & 1-5t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308725 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\,3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\,2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ t\\,i+(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+(1+{t}^{2})\\,k & =c(i+\\,3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\,2t\\,k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}t=c \\\\ 2+{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ 1+{t}^{2}=c(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain $t=c.$ Substituting $c=t$ into the second equality gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{3} \\\\ 2 & =2{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & =1 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis satisfies also the equation $1+{t}^{2}=c(2t).$\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =i+(2+{1}^{3})\\,j+(1+{1}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+2\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $P(1,3,2).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308733 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=0$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z=0\\cdot 1+0=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2=0-1+2=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z=(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2=\\frac{1}{2}-1+2=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,z(1)≈\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308734 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\c