{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,z(1)≈1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311545 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t-1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨{t}^{3}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩ & =c\\,⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ {e}^{t}=c{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t-1}=c{e}^{t-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we obtain that $c=1.$ Substituting $c=1$ into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}(t-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=0$, and $t=3.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{3},\\,{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{0-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,{e}^{-1}⟩ \\\\ r(3) & =⟨{3}^{3},\\,{e}^{3},\\,{e}^{3-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨27,\\,{e}^{3},\\,{e}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(0,1,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311546 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311547 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311548 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+5v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311555 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{-{t}^{2}x}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-2tx{e}^{-{t}^{2}x},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [#edb/ref 17592186311557 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{1+{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{3}{(x+t+1)^{4}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [#edb/ref 17592186311558 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)={e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [#edb/ref 17592186311578 "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+(v(t)+1)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1+v(t)+1-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-4 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+3(v(t)+1)-4 \\\\  & =2u(t)+2-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+3v(t)+3-4 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311580 "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t),\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-x(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+(v(t)+2)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2u(t)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+3(v(t)+2)-2 \\\\  & =2u(t)-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+3v(t)+6-2 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)-v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311581 "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)-1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+3x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)-1)^{2}+3(u(t)-1)+(v(t)+1)+1 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)-2u(t)+1)+3u(t)-3+v(t)+1+1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-4 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =-3(u(t)-1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+2(v(t)+1)-4 \\\\  & =-3u(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+2v(t)+2-4 \\\\  & =-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311582 "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+{y}^{2}(t)-3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+(v(t)+2)^{2}-3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1)+({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)-3u(t)-3-v(t)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+5y(t)-8 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+5(v(t)+2)-8 \\\\  & =2u(t)+2-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+5v(t)+10-8 \\\\  & =2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311583 "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)-1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+4x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)-1)^{2}+4(u(t)-1)+(v(t)+2)+1 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)-2u(t)+1)+4u(t)-4+v(t)+2+1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+8y(t)-10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)-1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+8(v(t)+2)-10 \\\\  & =2u(t)-2-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+8v(t)+16-10 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311596 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{4}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{4}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<4) & =P(X\\in {2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}(\\frac{1}{4})+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311597 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{8}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{x-1}(\\frac{3}{8}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<7) & =P(X\\in {3,4,5,6}) \\\\  & =P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{8})}^{2}(\\frac{3}{8})+{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{3}(\\frac{3}{8})+{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{4}(\\frac{3}{8})+{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{5}(\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & ≈0.3310.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311602 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-1=0 \\\\ 2x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding both equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n3x-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+2y-1=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311604 "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+3,\\,y(t)=v(t),\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-6x(t)+2y(t)+9 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+3)^{2}-6(u(t)+3)+2v(t)+9 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+6u(t)+9)-6u(t)-18+2v(t)+9 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+y(t)-12 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =4(u(t)+3)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)+12-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311613 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ x(4x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=2\\cdot 0=0.$\n\n- For $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get $y=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311615 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y=0 \\\\ x+y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-5y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311616 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2y-4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2y-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2(-x-2)-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+4-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\text{ }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=-0-2=-2.$\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=-(-2)-2=0.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311617 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y-1=0 \\\\ {y}^{3}+x-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=1-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}+x-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}+(1-y)-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ y(y-1)(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,\\text{ }0,\\text{ }1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $x:$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=1-0=1.$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get $x=1-1=0.$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get $x=1-(-1)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\text{and}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311722 "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,1,4)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ⟨{t}^{4},\\,{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩ & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4}=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ 4t=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, we get $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(1,1,4)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3},\\,2t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨4(1)^{3},\\,2(1),\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(1,1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨4,\\,2,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311735 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=8I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=8I+1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(8\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +8\\beta =4\\,