ops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=8I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=8I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +8\\