ratorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +3\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{4}{125}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{3}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{4}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{4}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=4,Q=3,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=4+3=7.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311805 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $50\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.004\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=50\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=50I=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nT