^{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=7, &  & b(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."] [#edb/ref 17592186314795 "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=3, &  & b(t)=-8, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [#edb/ref 17592186314807 "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}a(t) & b(t) \\\\ c(t) & d(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ g(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)-2t\\,{x}_{1}(t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)+5{x}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2t\\,{x}_{1}(t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,{x}_{2}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-5{x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2t & 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186314808 "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}a(t) & b(t) \\\\ c(t) & d(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ g(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186314809 "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}a(t) & b(t) \\\\ c(t) & d(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ g(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+4{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)=t \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+t \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186314814 "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-2, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=4, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=1, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=0, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."] [#edb/ref 17592186314816 "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-3, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6t) \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-2, &  & g(t)=2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=1, &  & b(t)=3, &  & f(t)=4{e}^{t} \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-4, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5), &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [#edb/ref 17592186314817 "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=3, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t) \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=4, &  & g(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-1, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=5, &  & d(t)=6, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=4, &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)={e}^{2} \\\\ c(t)=0, &  & d(t)=1, &  & g(t)=3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [#edb/ref 17592186314972 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-1=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186315000 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}$\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{11}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11$ is $F(s)=\\frac{11}{s},$ defined for $s>0.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186315001 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{{}_{0}}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{7}{3s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3s}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3}$ is $F(s)=-\\frac{7}{3s},$ defined for $s>0.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186315002 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{7t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{7t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st+7t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(7-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(7-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(7-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 7$ or $s=7.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(7-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7-s}{e}^{(7-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7}-\\frac{1}{s-7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>7, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<7.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(7) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(7-7)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s\\le 7$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s>7$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-7}-\\frac{1}{s-7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(7-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7}-\\frac{1}{s-7}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{7t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-7},$ defined for $s>7.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186315003 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st-t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne -1$ or $s=-1.$\n\n- If $s\\ne -1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-1-s}{e}^{(-1-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>-1, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-1) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-1-(-1))t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s\\le -1$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s>-1$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-1-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1},$ defined for $s>-1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186315004 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{t/4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{t/4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st+t/4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne \\frac{1}{4}$ or $s=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- If $s\\ne \\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{1/4-s}{e}^{(1/4-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1/4-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4}-\\frac{1}{s-1/4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{1}{4}) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/4-1/4)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s>\\frac{1}{4}$ the integral is convergent.\n\n- For $s\\le \\frac{1}{4}$ the integral is divergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4}-\\frac{1}{s-1/4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4}-\\frac{1}{s-1/4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{t/4}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{4}{4s-1},$ defined for $s>\\frac{1}{4}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186315005 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{t/2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st+t/2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne \\frac{1}{2}$ or $s=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- If $s\\ne \\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/2-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{1/2-s}{e}^{(1/2-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1/2-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/2}-\\frac{1}{s-1/2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/2-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{1}{2}) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/2-1/2)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0